# Project Conventions

## File and Directory Layout

```
apps/
  common/          # Abstract mixins and utils only — no concrete domain models
  users/           # User, UserProfile, auth API, signals, tokens, emails
  organizations/   # Organization model, admin, service
  <new_app>/       # Future domain apps
templates/emails/  # HTML + plain-text email templates
tests/
  conftest.py
  test_auth.py
  users/           # Profile and auth tests
  organizations/   # Org service and admin tests
.cursor/rules/     # Always-on agent conventions (this file)
```

## App Structure Rules

- Domain apps live under `apps/` and are registered in `LOCAL_APPS` in `config/settings/base.py`
- `apps/common` holds abstract models and utilities only — never concrete domain models
- One module per role: `models.py`, `views.py`, `admin.py`, `serializers.py`, `services.py`
- Split a module into a package (`models/__init__.py`, `models/foo.py`) only when the file exceeds ~250 lines or contains clearly unrelated classes

## Business Logic

- Complex create/update/email workflows belong in `services.py` — not in `ModelAdmin.save_model()` or API views
- Email send helpers belong in `emails.py`
- Admin and views call service functions — they do not contain the logic themselves
- Signals live in `signals.py` and are registered in `AppConfig.ready()`

## Imports

Always use absolute imports:
```python
from apps.users.models import User
from apps.organizations.services import create_org_with_central_admin
```

Never use relative imports across apps.

## Tests

- Tests live in the repo-root `tests/` directory, grouped by app name (`tests/users/`, `tests/organizations/`)
- Do NOT put tests inside `apps/`
- Ship pytest tests in the **same change** as the code — never leave tests for later
- Cover the happy path + the important failure cases for every new function/view/service
- Use `pytest.mark.django_db` and DRF's `APIClient` / fixtures from `tests/conftest.py`

## Documentation

- Every new module, public function, and class gets a short docstring (what it does and why)
- New or changed API endpoints are documented in **both**:
  - `FRONTEND_API.md` — request, success JSON, and error JSON
  - Swagger via `@extend_schema` / `@extend_schema_view` with success **and** error examples (use `apps.common.openapi`)
- If the change affects frontend behavior or payloads, also update `apps/common/frontend_guide.md` (React/Vite/TS examples). The live endpoint table on `/api/docs/frontend/` is generated from the schema.
- Do **not** ship an endpoint with tag-only `@extend_schema(tags=...)`
- Admin-only flows get a docstring on the service function
- Skip comments that only restate the code
- Follow `.cursor/rules/api-docs.mdc` for the full endpoint-docs checklist

## Git Workflow

- Commit after every meaningful unit of work with a short descriptive message
  Examples: `feat: add Organization model`, `feat: org admin add form`, `test: org service tests`
- Use conventional commit prefixes: `feat:`, `fix:`, `test:`, `docs:`, `refactor:`

## Terminal Commands (PowerShell)

- Use `;` to chain commands — NOT `&&` (PowerShell does not support `&&`)
- If a terminal command is rejected or a script fails to run, **stop immediately** and ask the user how to proceed — do not retry silently or work around it
